Key Idea According to conservation of energy, potential energy at height $h=$ kinetic energy at
ground. Potential energy = Kinetic energy
ie, m g h=$\frac{1}{2}$ m v^{2} \Rightarrow \quad v=$\sqrt{2 g h}$ If $h_{1}$ and $h_{2}$ are initial and final heights, then v_{1}=$\sqrt{2 g h_{1}}, \quad$ v_{2}=$\sqrt{2 g h_{2}}$ Loss in velocity $\Delta$ v=v_{1}-v_{2}=$\sqrt{2 g h_{1}}-\sqrt{2 g h_{2}}\therefore$ Fractional loss in velocity $=\frac{\Delta v}{v_{1}} \begin{array}{l}
=\frac{\sqrt{2 g h_{1}}-\sqrt{2 g h_{2}}}{\sqrt{2 g h_{1}}}$\\
=1-$\sqrt{\frac{h_{2}}{h_{1}}}
\end{array}$ Substituting the values, we have
